<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.1.3: Understanding Constraints</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00055</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.1.3: Understanding Constraints</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0742ed1d-3e54-43c0-b844-bfb0cc0fea42</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>A <term class="glossary-tooltip">constraint</term> is something that limits what is
        possible or reasonable in a situation.</para>
      <para>For example, one constraint in a pizza party might be the number of slices of pizza each
        person could have, \(s\). We can write \( s&lt;4 \) to say that each person gets fewer than
        4 slices.</para>
      <para>Look at the expressions you wrote when planning the pizza party earlier. Choose an
        expression that uses one or more letters. </para>
      \
      <exercise>
        <problem>
<para> What is the expression you are examining?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>

          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>

          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example.</para>

          <para>\( (1.50t + 21.99)p + d \)</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      
      <exercise>
        <problem>
<para>What does the first letter represent?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>

          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>

          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example.</para>

          <para>\(t\) = number of toppings</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      
      <exercise>
        <problem>
      <para>What values would be reasonable for this first letter? (For instance, could the value
          be greater than 50? Is it possible for the letter to be a non-whole number? A negative
          number?)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>

          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>

          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example.</para>

          <para>The cost of a topping is usually less than $2.00 per topping</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      
      <exercise>
        <problem>
  <para>What does the second letter represent? </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>

          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>

          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example.</para>

          <para>\(p\) = number of pizzas ordered</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What values would be reasonable for this second letter? (For instance, could the value
          be greater than 50? Is it possible for the letter to be a non-whole number? A negative
          number?) </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>

          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>

          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example.</para>

          <para>The number of pizzas ordered will probably be less than or equal to the number of
            students in the class. </para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write equations or inequalities that represent some constraints in your pizza party
          plan. If a quantity must be an exact value, use the = symbol. If it must be greater than
          or less than a certain value to be reasonable, use the \( &gt; \) or \( &lt; \) symbol.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>

          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>

          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example.</para>

          <para>\(t \leq 2.00 \)</para>

          <para>\(p \leq 30\) </para>

          <para> \(d \leq 5.00\) if \(d\) represents the delivery charge </para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
</section>
